! version = 2.0
! global utf8 = true

! var name = GenuaryBot
! var project = this Genuary-inspired generative art site

// --------------------------------------------------
// GREETINGS
// --------------------------------------------------

+ [*](hello|hi|hey|yo|hiya|sup)[*]
- Hello. I’m <bot name>. Ask me about the site, the prompts, the sketches, or the code.
- Hi. I’m <bot name>, the site chatbot for <bot project>.

+ good (morning|afternoon|evening)
- Hello. Ready to explore some generative stars?

// --------------------------------------------------
// IDENTITY
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (who are you|what are you|who is this|who is the bot)
- I’m <bot name>, a small rule-based chatbot added to this site.
- I’m <bot name>. I’m not a large AI model here on the page, just a scripted chatbot with predefined replies.

+ (are you an ai|are you intelligent|are you smart|are you a real ai)
- No. On the site, I’m a rule-based chatbot built with RiveScript.
- I’m not a full AI here. I match patterns in your message and reply with prewritten responses.

+ what can you do
- I can answer questions about the site, the prompts, the sketches, the tools used, and the overall project structure.
- I can help explain how the site works, what the daily pieces are about, and how the code is organized.

// --------------------------------------------------
// PROJECT
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (what is this site|what is this project|what is genuary|what is this about)
- This is a Genuary-inspired generative art site. Each day responds to a different prompt, and each sketch is built around a star-based visual system.
- The project is a gallery of daily generative pieces inspired by Genuary. The site presents them as fullscreen scrolling panels.

+ what is the concept
- The main concept is to reinterpret a shared star geometry across many different prompts, aesthetics, and systems.
- The project starts from a common star drawing structure, then transforms it into many different visual ideas across the 31 days.

+ why stars
- The star is the common visual thread across the whole project. It keeps the series coherent even when each day explores a very different prompt.
- Stars were chosen as the recurring shape so the daily prompts could stay connected while still changing style and behavior.

// --------------------------------------------------
// SITE STRUCTURE
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (how does the site work|how does this site work|how does this page work)
- The site creates a fullscreen scrolling gallery. Each panel corresponds to one day and mounts a p5.js sketch dynamically.
- The page is structured as scrollable sections. Each day has its own sketch file and is loaded into the corresponding panel.

+ (how is the code organized|how is the site organized|what is the code structure|what is the structure)
- The main page creates the gallery panels, then loads the daily sketches from files like js/sketches/day01.js, day02.js, and so on.
- The project is split into HTML, CSS, shared JavaScript utilities, and one sketch file per day inside js/sketches.

+ where are the sketches
- The daily sketches live in js/sketches/dayXX.js files.
- Each day is implemented in its own JavaScript file inside the js/sketches folder.

+ how are sketches loaded
- The site uses dynamic imports, so each day file is loaded when needed instead of being manually imported one by one.
- Each panel has a matching mount key like day01 or day12, and the site loads the corresponding sketch module automatically.

+ why does only one sketch run at a time
- Because the shared drawing bridge uses global bindings, keeping one active sketch at a time avoids conflicts between multiple p5 instances.
- The site mounts sketches dynamically and usually keeps one active at a time so the shared v3ga-style environment stays stable.

// --------------------------------------------------
// TOOLS / TECH
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (what tools does it use|what libraries does it use|what tech does it use|what languages does it use|what is it built with)
- The project uses HTML, CSS, vanilla JavaScript, p5.js, and the v3ga init_trace drawing helpers.
- The visual sketches are built with p5.js, while the site itself uses standard web technologies and a shared geometry system.

+ what is p5
- p5.js is a JavaScript library for creative coding. It makes it easier to draw, animate, and build interactive visual sketches in the browser.
- p5.js is the main drawing library used for the daily generative pieces on this site.

+ what is v3ga
- v3ga refers to the dessins geometriques et artistiques library used here through init_trace style helpers.
- The project uses a helper library inspired by geometric plotting workflows, especially for the shared star drawing logic.

// --------------------------------------------------
// PROMPTS / DAYS
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (how many days|how many sketches|how many prompts)
- The site is built as a 31-day series.
- There are 31 daily sketches in the project.

+ what is your favorite day
- I do not really have favorites, but the more system-based prompts are especially fun to explain.
- I’m biased toward the days where one rule creates a surprising result.

+ (what prompt is this|what is this day|what day is this)
- The visible panel should show the day number and prompt at the top of the screen.
- Each section of the gallery includes its day label and prompt in the interface overlay.

// --------------------------------------------------
// CHATBOT
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (how does the chatbot work|what is this chatbot|how are you made)
- I’m built with RiveScript, which is a rule-based chatbot scripting system.
- My replies come from a script file, not from live large-model reasoning inside the page.

+ why are you not ai
- Because this site requirement asks for a chatbot, and a rule-based one is lightweight, local, and easy to integrate into a static site.
- I’m intentionally simple. I’m here to explain the project, not to simulate a full assistant inside the browser.

// --------------------------------------------------
// INTERACTION
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (how do i use the site|how do i navigate|how do i explore)
- Scroll through the fullscreen sections to move from one day to another.
- Use the page scroll to move through the gallery. Each panel corresponds to a different daily prompt.

+ (is it interactive|can i interact|what can i click)
- Many sketches react to the mouse, and some also react to clicks or keys depending on the day.
- Several days include interaction such as mouse movement, clicking to regenerate, or toggling visual states.

+ how do i save
- Some drawing helpers support saving, but on this site the main goal is browsing the gallery rather than exporting every sketch directly.
- Saving depends on how a specific sketch was implemented. The site is mainly designed as an interactive gallery experience.

// --------------------------------------------------
// AUTHOR / PROCESS
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (how was this made|how did you make this|who made this)
- The project was assembled as a creative coding site with AI-assisted coding, manual editing, design choices, and prompt-by-prompt iteration.
- It combines a shared visual system, daily prompt interpretation, site design, and custom sketch logic.

+ was ai used
- Yes. AI-assisted coding was used as part of the process, alongside human direction, editing, and integration.
- The site combines human artistic direction with AI help for ideation and implementation.

// --------------------------------------------------
// DAY KEYWORDS
// --------------------------------------------------

+ (tell me about day 1|day 1)
- Day 1 interprets one color and one shape through a minimal star-based piece.
+ (tell me about day 2|day 2)
- Day 2 explores layering by stacking star echoes into a depth-based composition.
+ (tell me about day 3|day 3)
- Day 3 is built around the exact 42-line constraint.
+ (tell me about day 4|day 4)
- Day 4 turns the star into a low-resolution signal or pixel field.
+ (tell me about day 5|day 5)
- Day 5 reinterprets the star in isometric space as an architectural object.
+ (tell me about day 6|day 6)
- Day 6 switches between lights-on and lights-off states like a constellation blackout.
+ (tell me about day 7|day 7)
- Day 7 uses the star as a loading indicator and progress system.
+ (tell me about day 8|day 8)
- Day 8 uses chaotic motion to drive a star-based system.
+ (tell me about day 9|day 9)
- Day 9 renders the star as ASCII output.
+ (tell me about day 10|day 10)
- Day 10 follows the TAU-only code constraint.
+ (tell me about day 11|day 11)
- Day 11 reimagines the star as a woven textile motif.
+ (tell me about day 12|day 12)
- Day 12 turns the star system into a lava lamp atmosphere.
+ (tell me about day 13|day 13)
- Day 13 uses a simple turning rule to generate an unexpected path.
+ (tell me about day 14|day 14)
- Day 14 creates controlled wrongness: almost aligned, never quite perfect.
+ (tell me about day 15|day 15)
- Day 15 explores organic geometry through colony-like star growth.
+ (tell me about day 16|day 16)
- Day 16 uses recursive subdivision and stars inside terminal cells.
+ (tell me about day 17|day 17)
- Day 17 imagines the star as a living creature with pulse and limbs.
+ (tell me about day 18|day 18)
- Day 18 treats glitches as useful behavior rather than errors.
+ (tell me about day 19|day 19)
- Day 19 grows a seed into branches and star-blooms.
+ (tell me about day 20|day 20)
- Day 20 applies pixel sorting to a generated star scene.
+ (tell me about day 21|day 21)
- Day 21 builds the image using gradients only.
+ (tell me about day 22|day 22)
- Day 22 becomes a screensaver with bouncing star forms.
+ (tell me about day 23|day 23)
- Day 23 turns stars into architecture in a generated city.
+ (tell me about day 24|day 24)
- Day 24 uses Boolean logic between overlapping star regions.
+ (tell me about day 25|day 25)
- Day 25 shows mutation and inheritance across generations of stars.
+ (tell me about day 26|day 26)
- Day 26 uses pure black and white only, no gray.
+ (tell me about day 27|day 27)
- Day 27 generates its own palette and builds the piece from it.
+ (tell me about day 28|day 28)
- Day 28 turns the image into a broken glitch feed.
+ (tell me about day 29|day 29)
- Day 29 reveals the system as a debug overlay.
+ (tell me about day 30|day 30)
- Day 30 grows a garden of star-plants.
+ (tell me about day 31|day 31)
- Day 31 is the finale, a singularity and rebirth cycle.

